      "text": "To find the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to determine the smallest positive value \\( T \\) such that \\( y(x + T) = y(x) \\) for all \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, recall the definitions of the tangent and cotangent functions:\n\\[\n\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cot x = \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}\n\\]\n\nThus, the given function can be rewritten as:\n\\[\ny(x) = \\tan x + \\cot x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}\n\\]\n\nWe can combine these fractions over a common denominator:\n\\[\ny(x) = \\frac{\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x}{\\sin x \\cos x}\n\\]\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity \\(\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1\\), we get:\n\\[\ny(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sin x \\cos x}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify \\(\\sin x \\cos x\\) using the double-angle identity:\n\\[\n\\sin x \\cos x = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2x\n\\]\n\nThus, the function becomes:\n\\[\ny(x) = \\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2x} = \\frac{2}{\\sin 2x}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the period \\( T \\) of \\( y(x) = \\frac{2}{\\sin 2x} \\). The function \\(\\sin 2x\\) has a period of \\(\\pi\\) because the period of \\(\\sin kx\\) is \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{k}\\). Here, \\( k = 2 \\), so:\n\\[\n\\text{Period of } \\sin 2x = \\frac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi\n\\]\n\nSince the period of \\(\\sin 2x\\) is \\(\\pi\\), the period of \\(\\frac{2}{\\sin 2x}\\) will also be \\(\\pi\\) because the presence of the constant 2 does not affect the period. Therefore, the period of \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pi}\n\\]",
