      "text": "To determine the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to understand the periods of the individual functions \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\) and then find their least common multiple (LCM), because the period of the sum of two periodic functions is the least common multiple of their periods.\n\n1. **Period of \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\):**\n   - The function \\(\\tan x\\) has a period of \\(\\pi\\), i.e., \\(\\tan(x + \\pi) = \\tan x\\).\n   - Similarly, the function \\(\\cot x\\) also has a period of \\(\\pi\\), i.e., \\(\\cot(x + \\pi) = \\cot x\\).\n\n2. **Finding the LCM of the periods:**\n   - Since both \\(\\tan x\\) and \\(\\cot x\\) have a period of \\(\\pi\\), the period of their sum \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) will be the least common multiple of \\(\\pi\\) and \\(\\pi\\), which is \\(\\pi\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion:**\n   - The period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is \\(\\pi\\).\n\nTherefore, the period of \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\pi}\\).",
